Let me give you clear, exam-ready steps for B1 (creating a Docker image from a local application and running it).

---

B1: Create Docker image from local app & run it

What you need:

· A simple Java application (e.g., HelloWorld.java)
· Docker installed and running
· A Dockerfile

---

Step 1: Create the Java application

Create a folder, e.g., myapp. Inside it, create HelloWorld.java:

```java
public class HelloWorld {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello from Docker!");
    }
}
```

---

Step 2: Create a Dockerfile (same folder)

Create a file named Dockerfile (no extension) with this content:

```dockerfile
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jdk
WORKDIR /app
COPY HelloWorld.java .
RUN javac HelloWorld.java
CMD ["java", "HelloWorld"]
```

Why eclipse-temurin:21-jdk?
The old openjdk:11 is deprecated. This is a modern, maintained alternative.

---

Step 3: Build the Docker image

Open a terminal/command prompt inside the myapp folder and run:

```bash
docker build -t my-java-app:latest .
```

· -t my-java-app:latest gives the image a name and tag
· The . means "use the Dockerfile in the current directory"

Expected output: Successfully built ... and tagged my-java-app:latest

---

Step 4: Run the container from the image

```bash
docker run --name my-container my-java-app:latest
```

· --name my-container gives the container a name
· my-java-app:latest is the image name

Expected output: Hello from Docker!

---

Step 5: Verify the container ran (optional)

```bash
docker ps -a
```

You’ll see my-container with status Exited (0) – that means success.

---

What if you want to run the container in interactive mode?

If you need to open a shell inside the container:

```bash
docker run -it my-java-app:latest /bin/bash
```

But for B1, just running the application is enough.

---

Quick checklist for exam:

Action Command
Build image docker build -t my-java-app:latest .
Run container docker run --name my-container my-java-app:latest
See running containers docker ps
Stop container docker stop my-container
Remove container docker rm my-container
Remove image docker rmi my-java-app:latest

---

Common mistakes to avoid:

❌ Using openjdk:11 → use eclipse-temurin:21-jdk instead
❌ Forgetting the . at the end of docker build
❌ Running docker run without building the image first
❌ Docker daemon not running → start Docker Desktop before building

